#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "9f96c35d-28bb-533d-98d0-8608b791c8e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5513" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$. The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & T(b_{1})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{2}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} - R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823a7d28-5a92-5901-8ee0-e5116df6766b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Image of a Vector Given the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "a2b2d84f-cf3b-52a7-98e0-e659d04697f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5514" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The image of $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{B} &= [T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22ca4f81-6fec-5822-b281-5ce1e23d399e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Three-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "d46f548c-c15c-5716-9f37-7f9dfa751f35" :question/math-academy-id "e-5515" :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$. The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & b_{3} & T(b_{1}) & T(b_{2})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{3} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 3R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, $a=1$, $b=-2$, $c=-2$ and\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-2)+(-2) = -3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "71ac5a84-d8e8-5de6-8adc-e0172457a772" :question/math-academy-id "e-7249" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$. The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & c \\\\ 0 & b & 1 \\\\ a & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that\n\n$T(b_{1})|= b_{1} + b_{3},; T(b_{2})|= b_{1} + b_{2},; T(b_{3})|= b_{2} + b_{3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1$, $b=1$, $c=0$ and\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823a7d28-5a92-5901-8ee0-e5116df6766b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Image of a Vector Given the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6eaf97a-8045-54c2-acc2-d246d0ddfdc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123233} {:answer/id #uuid "c1862a90-335e-5028-99a4-c24e019b8410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123234} {:answer/id #uuid "5281ecb6-a51d-5aaa-b720-a0505ae1dd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123235} {:answer/id #uuid "58d8cb6c-9b33-5901-935c-28144b7d5cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123236} {:answer/id #uuid "78c4cca3-38b3-565c-a788-42b851217f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78c4cca3-38b3-565c-a788-42b851217f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123237} :answer-field/id #uuid "9283542c-c712-536f-ac32-339a1626972f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123232}] :question/id #uuid "41e58550-d329-5f5e-b63a-6c321a9666b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38087" :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n\n$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "56260e5b-1f89-58db-8833-b8efa77b5e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis in a Two-Dimensional Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d79f85ba-7cb2-52b3-ada0-bb484a2a6ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 10 & 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123251} {:answer/id #uuid "22f5d8f9-b0f1-55f2-9e0d-60700f80d0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123252} {:answer/id #uuid "27595f53-4d57-52be-b45b-e46b3bdeb1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123253} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9856f8-b77e-5751-9d60-30d12468007a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -1 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123254} {:answer/id #uuid "53165651-79d1-5a41-a434-d25aba194674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -13 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f5d8f9-b0f1-55f2-9e0d-60700f80d0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123252} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea9c2500-a93a-5716-acca-05b6e8f6ddd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123250}] :question/id #uuid "20b7ce30-b743-5df5-84bf-bcc3d671ace4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38188" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T]_{B}$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$, $T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and $B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$."}]}
